A||||9||Rodney||Broken rear hinge strap repair kit||||1||
Z||000000||Rodney||07-28-2001||05:31 PM||||I have repair kits for the rear deck lid hinge straps. I have been trying to make a bolt on kit but I have not been able to come up with a design that is strong enough and easy to bolt on. But I do have repair kits ready now to replace the OEM cables. It requires you to drill out the factory rivets that hold the old straps to the hinge. This can all be done with the hinges and factory springs in the car. It is not hard to drill out the old rivets. Just center punch in the center of the head and drill until they fall off using a 1/4" drill bit. You then drill the existing hole slightly larger and tap to 5/16" x 18. The new straps just bolt in with my special bolt with a shoulder. All done. <P>The price per strap kit is probably going to be $12.50 each. Both should be replaced at the same time. The R&D samples I have are plated steel eyelets. The factory straps have stainless steel ends. I can go to stainless steel ends but the price would go to $16 each. I have to do it one way or the other. The minimum order on the straps probably prevents me from doing both. <P>I also hope to offer a kit that has OEM similar rivets that can be swegged in from the back side. This kit would require the removal of the hinge. Cost would be about $24 each with the stainless steel ends. $18.50 with the plated ends.<P>Need input on using the stainless steel eyelets or the plated eyelets. <P>Thanks,<BR>Rodney Dickman<BR>New web page! <A HREF="http://www.rodneydickman.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.rodneydickman.com</A> <P><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000001||deceler8||07-28-2001||07:25 PM||||No recommendation, but a question...<P>DO you have or are you planning to produce dash corner repair pieces ?<P>Seems I asked you about that last year in Onawa and you were planning to produce them.<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/deceler8_sig.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||AkursedX||07-28-2001||08:20 PM||||I'd say to go with the stainless steel.  It's not too much more expensive.  (I would pay it at least)  and it'll last alot longer.<P>BTW, this is a great idea!  I tried to fix my broken strap before, and it was quite the pain.  It kinda works, but if I could get a replacement, I definietly would.<P>------------------<BR><BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sigpic2_2.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||Leadfoot||07-28-2001||09:04 PM||||If I had to buy a pair today I would probably order the stainless steel ones.  Rodney, thanks for the fast turn around on the subwoofer kit.  I didn't expect it until monday. U D MAN!!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/cool.gif"><P>||||reg||1
Z||000004||AutoXer||07-28-2001||09:25 PM||||Definately stainless.  It is worth the couple bucks extra.<P>btw:  I need a kit for my Formula as soon as they are available.||||reg||10
Z||000005||Will||07-28-2001||10:57 PM||||I put a whole bunch of your stuff in today, Rodney: shift linkage kit, power window kit, new cruise control stalk (cruise still doesn't work). I also got a new rack bushing and trans side bearings to go with my new Torsen.<BR>Great service and selection.<P>My vote is for the stainless, although I'm not sure if it counts since I'll be going to gas struts shortly after I get my N* in (or maybe shortly after I get tired of carrying around a piece of 1" dowel rod to prop up the decklid  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> ).||||reg||1
Z||000006||theogre||07-29-2001||12:07 AM||||Mine are ok now.... If/when I need them I'd prefer the SS ones. It's not just an issue of how long they last. Plain steel would last a long time but no mater how it's finished it will eventually rust when the finish wears thru. The rust gets onto other things and just plain looks bad.<P>Unless people are doing a show car the bolts should work fine. Are the bolts SS or mild steel? Mild steel bolts would have the same ugly corossion problems as cable ends would.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000007||Rodney||07-29-2001||08:31 AM||||The bolts are stainless steel. When I offer the OEM style rivits they will also be stainless steel. I'm going to look at making the cable ends stainless steel. Problem is I have to order 1000 cables per order for the stainless. If I use the plated ends I can order 200 per order. ||||reg||1
Z||000008||theogre||07-29-2001||03:47 PM||||Ouch. I'm not sure what kind of volume you'd do in them. 1000 units could be hard to move. <P>For safety, I think you should sell them as pairs only. That is really the only way to reliably do this repair. If one cable broke then likely the other isn't far behind. Even then that's 500 sets to move compared to 100 of the plated sets.<P>As long as the bolt/rivit is stainless and the cable ends are plated well, only people like me who are constantly in the engine bay, and maybe people in the salt belt, should have a rust issue. A good thing with the bolts is that if they do get rust they can be cleaned or replaced easy enough.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Rodney||07-31-2001||11:49 AM||||I have decided to go with both the plated ends and the stainless steel ends straps. That way someone who does not care about them being correct will be able to fix their rear straps for much less. ||||reg||1
